    "Human: You are an expert L1 IT Support Automation Engineer and OKF Playbook Architect.\n\nYour task is to analyze a raw human SOP document (which may contain GUI click instructions, manual steps, or text descriptions) and convert it into a structured, execution-ready OKF Playbook JSON schema for automated agent execution.\n\n## REGISTERED API TOOL PLATFORMS OVERVIEW\nBelow are the registered IT Tool Platforms available in the system:\n- Platform: 'mock' (Provider: 'l1_testing_platform_api') — L1 Support Platform REST API client — covers user auth, password reset/change, MFA reset/enable/verify, account lock/unlock, user search, and audit logs.\n- Platform: 'auth0' (Provider: 'auth0_user_management') — Auth0 User Identity Management tools for searching users, triggering password reset emails, and unlocking accounts.\n- Platform: 'email' (Provider: 'email_service') — Email and communication service for sending ticket updates, resolution tokens, and notifications to users.\n- Platform: 'airretailer' (Provider: 'airretailer_vpn_rdp_tools') — AirRetailer VPN and Remote Desktop (RDP) tools — VPN portal login, Cisco AnyConnect client installer/settings, VPN connection, and RDP session setup.\n- Platform: 'solandra group' (Provider: 'solandra group management') — Ticketing system used to review escalated password-reset requests and record the final resolution and audit note.\n\nDYNAMIC TOOL INSPECTION RULES:\nYou have access to callable dynamic tools to inspect tool details on-demand:\n- `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)`: Fetch the exact functions, required inputs, and response schemas for a specific platform (e.g., 'mock', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer').\n- `list_available_platforms()`: Get the summary list of all registered platforms.\n\nSTRICT TOOL-CALLING MANDATE:\n1. Call `get_platform_tools` at most ONCE for any relevant candidate platform at the start of conversion. `get_platform_tools` returns ALL functions, input parameters, and schemas for that platform in a single payload.\n2. DO NOT call `get_platform_tools` repeatedly or per individual step.\n3. DO NOT call `list_available_platforms` if you already see the platform list above.\n4. If the raw SOP only involves native OS/system CLI commands (e.g. `chkdsk`, `ipconfig`, `sfc`, `taskkill`, `ping`) and does not match a registered platform, make ZERO (0) tool calls and proceed directly to outputting the Command Playbook JSON.\n\n## 8-STAGE DEEP CoT REASONING MANDATE (WITH 4 MANDATORY VERIFICATION GATES)\nYou MUST follow these 8 reasoning stages in exact numerical order:\n\n1. **Stage 1 (Dynamic Tool-Catalog Lookup & 4 Mandatory Verification Gates)**:\n   - If the raw SOP describes actions matching a registered platform (e.g. 'auth0', 'mock', 'email', 'airretailer'), invoke `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)` ONCE to retrieve all active tool functions and parameter schemas.\n   - Inspect the retrieved functions against the raw SOP using these 4 strict Verification Gates:\n     * **Gate 1 (Exact Function Match)**: Does the raw SOP action match an explicit function name declared in the retrieved tools?\n     * **Gate 2 (Active Provider Match)**: Is the provider name active and registered in the catalog?\n     * **Gate 3 (Problem Alignment)**: Does the candidate tool function solve the exact problem described in the raw SOP?\n     * **Gate 4 (Parameter Completeness)**: Are all 'required_inputs' declared by the tool function present or mappable to entities?\n\n    * DECISION MANDATE:\n      - IF ALL 4 Gates PASS -> Classify as a Tool Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"tool_call\"`). Populate `\"provider\"`, `\"function_name\"`, and `\"comm_inputs\"`.\n      - IF ANY Gate FAILS -> Classify as a Command Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"command\"`). Translate GUI/text steps into exact Windows/Linux CLI shell commands with `\"shell\": \"powershell\"`.\n      - STRICT STEP TYPE RULE: `\"step_type\"` MUST strictly be either `\"tool_call\"` or `\"command\"`. NEVER use `\"verification\"`, `\"communication\"`, or any other custom step_type string.\n\n    * PER-STEP REASONING MANDATE: For EVERY step, populate `\"step_reasoning\"` with a concise (2-4 sentence) explanation of why THIS specific output was chosen for THIS step, written for a human reviewer auditing the conversion. It MUST cover:\n      - Which of the 4 Verification Gates passed or failed, and why (i.e. why `tool_call` vs `command` was chosen here).\n      - If `tool_call`: why this exact `provider`/`function_name` was selected over any other catalog entry that looked plausible, and where each `comm_inputs` value came from (raw SOP text, an inferred entity, or a prior step's `output_variable`).\n      - If `command`: why this exact CLI command (and any `{{{{entities.*}}}}`/`{{{{variables.*}}}}` placeholders) was the correct translation of the SOP instruction.\n      - This is per-step, evidence-based reasoning distinct from the global `reasoning_chain` (which only summarizes each of the 8 stages once for the whole playbook).\n\n2. **Stage 2 (Diagnostic & Remediation Sequence Ordering)**: Ensure non-destructive search/diagnostic steps (e.g. `search_users` or `ipconfig`) come FIRST before any corrective or account modification commands.\n\n3. **Stage 3 (Precondition & Variable Extraction)**: Identify dynamic placeholders and ENFORCE strict double-brace format:\n   - For `command` steps: Insert placeholders in command text (e.g. `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.executable}}}}`).\n   - For `tool_call` steps: You MUST populate all required input parameters mentioned in the SOP (e.g. `current_password`, `email`, `user_id`) inside `comm_inputs` (e.g. `{{{{ \"password\": \"{{{{entities.current_password}}}}\" }}}}`).\n   - Use `{{{{entities.var_name}}}}` for pre-configured entities (`{{{{entities.user_id}}}}`, `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.email}}}}`, `{{{{entities.current_password}}}}`) and `{{{{variables.var_name}}}}` for runtime step outputs. DO NOT use un-nested single braces.\n\n4. **Stage 4 (Strict SOP Command Fidelity & Zero Unwritten Action Hallucinations)**: Extract ONLY actions and tools explicitly written in the raw SOP text. DO NOT invent, fabricate, or add unwritten steps (such as `send_email`, `search_users`, or notification steps) unless explicitly requested in the raw SOP text. If not explicitly specified in the raw SOP, set `\"fallback_command\": null`.\n\n5. **Stage 5 (Verification & Output Parsing Instructions)**: Verification and diagnostic check steps described in the raw SOP MUST NOT be created as separate step types. Instead, create a `tool_call` or `command` step and write clear regex/string inspection rules for `output_parse_instruction` and `expected_output_contains`:\n   - For `command` steps: Inspect CLI stdout (e.g. \"If output contains 'SUCCESS', set 'kill_confirmed' to 'true'\").\n   - For `tool_call` steps: Use the exact JSON field names declared in the tool's `Response Schema` (e.g. \"If response contains status='unlocked', set 'unlock_success' to 'true' and store response.user_id in variables.user_id\").\n\n6. **Stage 6 (100% Diagnostic Step Recall Mandate)**: You MUST extract EVERY SINGLE step described in the procedure section of the raw SOP text. Omitting any diagnostic or verification step is STRICTLY PROHIBITED.\n\n7. **Stage 7 (Conditional Branch Mapping & execute_if)**: If the raw SOP specifies conditional skip instructions, map this logic into valid `execute_if` boolean expressions (e.g., `execute_if: variables.account_found == 'true'`).\n\n8. **Stage 8 (Dynamic Timeout Allocation by Action Scope)**: Assign `timeout_seconds` dynamically: Quick Info Read / API Call (30s), System Scan / Diagnostic Audit (90s), Full Repair / Heavy Job (300s).\n\n9. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit GUI Window Setup Steps)**: DO NOT generate non-executable GUI setup steps like \"Open Command Prompt\" or \"Open Web Browser\". Start directly with functional actions.\n\n10. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Zero Unwritten Action Addition)**: You are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN from inventing or adding steps/tool calls (such as `send_email` or `search_users`) that are NOT explicitly stated in the raw SOP document.\n\n11. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit Manual Human/Verbal Confirmation Steps)**: Human/verbal/ticket confirmation checks, manual admin approvals, and user clarification steps are currently PAUSED. Omit non-automated verbal/ticket confirmation steps and start directly with automated API tool calls (`tool_call`) and CLI system commands (`command`).\n\n\n## OUTPUT JSON SCHEMA MANDATE\nCRITICAL FORMATTING MANDATE:\n- All keys and string values MUST be enclosed in standard DOUBLE QUOTES (\").\n- NEVER use Python single quotes (') for dictionary keys or strings.\n- Return raw, valid JSON only matching this schema:\n```json\n{{\n    \"reasoning_chain\": [\n        \"[Stage 1 Verification Gates]: Gate 1 (Function match) -> Gate 2 (Provider active) -> Gate 3 (Problem aligned) -> Gate 4 (Params complete) -> Result: tool_call | command\",\n        \"[Stage 2 Sequencing]: Ordered search/diagnostic steps before remediation\",\n        \"[Stage 3 Variables]: Formatted {{{{entities.var}}}} placeholders\",\n        \"[Stage 4 Fallbacks]: Mapped fallback actions\",\n        \"[Stage 5 Verification]: Added output parse instructions\",\n        \"[Stage 6 Recall]: Preserved 100% of diagnostic steps\",\n        \"[Stage 7 Branching]: Mapped execute_if conditions\",\n        \"[Stage 8 Timeouts]: Assigned timeout_seconds\"\n    ],\n    \"sop_id\": \"SOP-{{file_name_slug}}-001\",\n    \"title\": \"Title of Playbook\",\n    \"category\": \"network | storage | system | logins | software | hardware\",\n    \"target_os\": \"windows | linux\",\n    \"description\": \"Comprehensive overview of what this playbook does\",\n    \"required_entities\": [\"user_id\", \"email\", \"hostname\"],\n    \"trigger_words\": [\"unlock\", \"account\", \"login\"],\n    \"steps\": [\n        {{\n            \"step_number\": 1,\n            \"sop_step_description\": \"Detailed step objective\",\n            \"step_type\": \"tool_call | command\",\n            \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 result and why tool_call/command was chosen; why this exact provider/function/command and comm_inputs/placeholders were used over alternatives.\",\n            \"platform\": \"high-level vendor/platform inferred from SOP or tool catalog (only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"provider\": \"active_provider_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"function_name\": \"exact_function_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"comm_inputs\": {{\n                \"user_id\": \"{{{{entities.user_id}}}}\"\n            }},\n            \"command\": \"ipconfig /all (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"shell\": \"powershell (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"fallback_command\": null,\n            \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n            \"expected_output_contains\": \"unlocked\",\n            \"timeout_seconds\": 30,\n            \"output_variable\": \"step_result\",\n            \"output_parse_instruction\": \"Inspect output/JSON response and set status flags.\",\n            \"execute_if\": null,\n            \"is_dangerous\": false,\n            \"danger_warning\": null\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n```\n\n## RAW SOP DOCUMENT TEXT TO TRANSLATE:\nReset a Locked-Out User's Password via CoreID Admin Console\nL1 Escalation Runbook — Solandra Corp IT Support\nsop_id\nSOL-PWD-RESET-L1-002\nresolution_type\nescalate_only\naudience\nL1 Support Agent (authorized admin)\nrequired_role\nCoreID: Password Reset Admin\norg\nSolandra Corp\napplication\nCoreID Admin Console (mock)\nPurpose\nWhen a user's self-service password reset fails or the user has no working verification channel, the ticket escalates to L1. An authorized L1 agent with the Password Reset Admin role in CoreID resets the password directly from the admin console.\nBecause the agent's own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and role-gated, this flow does not send a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a ticketing system, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The main content area is divided into two sections: a header and a body.\n\n**Header:**\n\n* A light gray bar at the top with the text \"Signed in as: A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\" in small font.\n* A dark blue sidebar on the left with the following items:\n\t+ \"CoreID\" in white text at the top\n\t+ \"Admin Console\" in smaller white text below\n\t+ A list of menu items in white text, including \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", \"Settings\", and \"Opened\"\n\n**Body:**\n\n* A large white box in the center of the page with the title \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" in bold black text.\n* Below the title, there are several lines of text that provide information about the ticket, including:\n\t+ \"Category: Password Reset - Escalated\"\n\t+ \"Requester: J. Employee\"\n\t+ \"Employee ID: SOL-04821\"\n\t+ \"Manager: A. Menon\"\n\t+ \"Department: Finance\"\n\t+ \"Opened: Today, 10:42 AM\"\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of a ticketing system, specifically a ticket with the number SOL-84213. The ticket is related to a password reset issue and has been escalated. The requester is J. Employee, and the manager is A. Menon. The ticket was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n* The application or website is not explicitly identified, but it appears to be a custom-built ticketing system.\n* The current screen shows a single ticket with various details, including category, requester, employee ID, manager, department, and opened date and time.\n* The overall layout is clean and simple, with clear headings and concise text.\n* There are no buttons, menus, or fields visible on this screen.\n* The background is white, and the text is black.\n* There are no unusual visual conditions or changes visible on this screen.\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n* Ticket #SOL-84213\n* Category: Password Reset - Escalated\n* Requester: J. Employee\n* Employee ID: SOL-04821\n* Manager: A. Menon\n* Department: Finance\n* Opened: Today, 10:42 AM\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n* None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n* Category: Software/Application\n* Severity: Medium\n* Reason: The issue appears to be related to a password reset, which is a common problem in software applications.\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n* OBSERVED FACTS:\n\t+ The ticket number is SOL-84213.\n\t+ The category is Password Reset - Escalated.\n\t+ The requester is J. Employee.\n\t+ The employee ID is SOL-04821.\n\t+ The manager is A. Menon.\n\t+ The department is Finance.\n\t+ The ticket was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n* INFERENCE:\n\t+ The issue is likely related to a password reset problem in the software application.\n\t+ The escalation of the issue suggests that it may be a critical or high-priority problem.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, and the text is legible. There are no missing or unclear elements that would affect the accuracy of the interpretation.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user's information displayed on the screen. The user's name is A. Rao, and their role is L1 Support - Password Reset Admin.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing user accounts and permissions.\n    *   It provides a centralized location for administrators to view and manage user information, including names, roles, and permissions.\n*   **User Information**\n    *   The user's name is A. Rao.\n    *   Their role is L1 Support - Password Reset Admin.\n    *   The user's ID is SOL-04821.\n*   **Password Reset Admin**\n    *   The user has the role of Password Reset Admin, which allows them to reset passwords for other users.\n    *   This role is typically used by IT administrators or help desk staff to manage user passwords and access.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, displaying user information for A. Rao, who has the role of L1 Support - Password Reset Admin. The user's ID is SOL-04821, and they have the ability to reset passwords for other users.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.80 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The sidebar has a list of options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\". The main content area displays a form titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" with two checkboxes: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\". Below these checkboxes are two more options: \"Department matches record\" and \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\". The form also includes a note at the bottom stating \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a form for identity verification of an employee. The form has several checkboxes and options, and a note indicating that two out of four requirements have been met.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n* The application is the CoreID Admin Console.\n* The current screen is a form for identity verification of an employee.\n* The form has several checkboxes and options, including \"Employee ID matches ticket\", \"Manager name matches ticket\", \"Department matches record\", and \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n* The form also includes a note at the bottom stating \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n* None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n* None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n* Category: Unknown\n* Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n* OBSERVED FACTS:\n\t+ The form has several checkboxes and options.\n\t+ The form includes a note at the bottom stating \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n* INFERENCE:\n\t+ The form is likely used for identity verification of an employee.\n\t+ The checkboxes and options suggest that the form is checking for various pieces of information to confirm the employee's identity.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the text is not visible, making it difficult to determine the exact purpose of the form.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The sidebar has a list of options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\". The main content area displays a form with a title \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" and a temporary password field. Below the title, there is a checkbox labeled \"Force change at next sign-in\". At the bottom of the form, there is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n\nHere are the main points from the image:\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**: The top-left corner of the image shows the CoreID Admin Console logo.\n* **Sidebar Options**: The sidebar has several options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n* **Form Title**: The form title is \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n* **Temporary Password Field**: The form has a temporary password field with a placeholder text \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\".\n* **Force Change at Next Sign-in Checkbox**: Below the title, there is a checkbox labeled \"Force change at next sign-in\".\n* **Generate Temporary Password Button**: At the bottom of the form, there is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n\nBased on the image, it appears that the user is attempting to reset their password for the J. Employee account. The form provides a temporary password and allows the user to force a change at the next sign-in. The image does not show any error messages or warnings.\n\n**Summary**: The image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a form for resetting the password of the J. Employee account. The form provides a temporary password and allows the user to force a change at the next sign-in.\n\n**Detailed Description**:\n\n* **Application**: The application is the CoreID Admin Console.\n* **Current Screen**: The current screen is the password reset form for the J. Employee account.\n* **Layout**: The layout is a simple form with a title, temporary password field, checkbox, and button.\n* **UI Elements**: The UI elements include the form title, temporary password field, checkbox, and button.\n* **Highlighted Areas**: There are no highlighted areas in the image.\n\n**Extracted Text**:\n\n* **Form Title**: \"Reset Password - J. Employee\"\n* **Temporary Password Field**: \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\"\n* **Checkbox Label**: \"Force change at next sign-in\"\n* **Button Text**: \"Generate Temporary Password\"\n\n**Errors and Warnings**:\n\n* **None Visible**\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**:\n\n* **Category**: Software/Application\n* **Severity**: Low\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**:\n\n* **Observed Facts**:\n\t+ The image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console.\n\t+ The form title is \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n\t+ The temporary password field has a placeholder text \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\".\n\t+ The checkbox is labeled \"Force change at next sign-in\".\n\t+ The button text is \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n* **Inference**:\n\t+ The user is attempting to reset their password for the J. Employee account.\n\t+ The form provides a temporary password and allows the user to force a change at the next sign-in.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**:\n\n* **Confidence Score**: 0.95\n* **Confidence Level**: High\n* **Confidence Reason**: The image is clear and well-lit, and the text is legible.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   Ticket Number: SOL-84213\n    *   Status: Resolved\n*   **Resolution Notes**\n    *   Identity verified (2 of 4 checks)\n    *   Temporary password issued via CoreID\n    *   Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled\n    *   No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy\n\nThe image provides clear information about the ticket resolution, including the ticket number, status, and resolution notes. The resolution notes indicate that the identity was verified, a temporary password was issued, and force-change-at-next-sign-in was enabled. Additionally, it is noted that no OTP was sent, and the reset was admin-mediated per policy.\n\n**Summary**: The image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console with a ticket resolution page open, displaying the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n**Detailed Description**: The image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes. The ticket number is SOL-84213, and the status is Resolved. The resolution notes indicate that the identity was verified, a temporary password was issued, and force-change-at-next-sign-in was enabled. Additionally, it is noted that no OTP was sent, and the reset was admin-mediated per policy.\n\n**Extracted Text**: None\n\n**Errors and Warnings**: None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**:\n\n*   **Observed Facts**:\n    *   Ticket number: SOL-84213\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution notes: Identity verified, temporary password issued, force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled, no OTP sent, admin-mediated reset per policy\n*   **Inference**: The ticket was resolved, and the identity was verified. A temporary password was issued, and force-change-at-next-sign-in was enabled. Additionally, no OTP was sent, and the reset was admin-mediated per policy.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**: CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95 CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, and the text is legible.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at